1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,030
Have you ever bumped into someone and their name just doesn't come to you?
你是否曾经意外撞见某人却想不起他们的名字？

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:09,560
Or, you walk into a room and suddenly can't recall why you're there?
或者，你走进一个房间却突然想不起自己为什么在那里？

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:14,580
Being forgetful can be really annoying, sometimes even embarrassing.
健忘可能真的很烦人，有时甚至令人尴尬。

4
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:19,630
But what if I told you that there are actually good reasons for our memories fading away?
但如果我告诉你，我们的记忆逐渐消逝其实是有充分理由的呢？

5
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:25,890
In a BBC interview, neuroscientist Charan Ranganath uses a hoarding analogy.
在一次BBC采访中，神经科学家查兰·兰加纳特使用了一个囤积的类比。

6
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:29,900
We don't tend to store everything we've ever owned in our house.
我们不会把曾经拥有的所有东西都储存在房子里。

7
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,340
Similarly, we don't need to store all our memories either.
同样，我们也不需要储存所有的记忆。

8
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:41,760
He says, "If we didn't forget anything, we'd be hoarding memories, and you'd never be able to find what you want, when you want it."
他说："如果我们什么都不忘记，我们就会囤积记忆，你就永远无法在你需要的时候找到你想要的东西。"

9
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:51,280
For example, you don't need to remember the hotel number weeks after you've left the hotel or memorise all the faces you pass on the street.
例如，你不需要在离开酒店几周后还记得酒店房间号，也不需要记住你在街上经过的所有面孔。

10
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:57,540
We also need to be able to forget things in order to update our memories with new information.
我们也需要能够遗忘事物，以便用新信息更新我们的记忆。

11
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:06,620
Imagine you get used to the same school timetable or work schedule for a whole year, but the next year, you have new procedures or routines.
想象一下，你一整年都习惯了同样的学校课程表或工作日程表，但第二年，你有了新的规程或日常活动。

12
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:13,270
Your brain needs to be flexible and forget irrelevant details in order to handle the new information.
你的大脑要灵活，并遗忘无关的细节，以便处理新信息。

13
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:17,890
Or, maybe it's been 10 years since you last saw a distant relative.
或者，也许你上一次见到一位远亲已经是十年前了。

14
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:23,850
It's likely their face has changed a lot since then, so that memory needs to be updated as well.
从那以后，他们的脸很可能发生了很大变化，所以那段记忆也需要更新。

15
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:30,680
And, if you've ever been convinced that you know a word, but it just doesn't pop into your head, you're not alone.
而且，如果你曾经确信自己知道一个词，但就是想不起来，你并不孤单。

16
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:38,660
This tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon may suggest that some things are not fully forgotten, just currently inaccessible.
这种话到嘴边却说不出来的现象可能表明，有些东西并没有被完全遗忘，只是暂时无法访问。

17
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:52,000
Those with a higher level of knowledge are more affected by this, according to studies such as 'Impact of knowledge and age on tip-of-the-tongue rates', because their brains have to sort through more information to find the word.
根据《知识和年龄对话到嘴边现象发生率的影响》等研究，知识水平较高的人受此影响更大，因为他们的大脑需要筛选更多信息才能找到那个词。

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,670
Memories are sometimes compared to a cliff: once they have crumbled, they are lost and cannot be retrieved.
记忆有时被比作悬崖：一旦崩塌，就永远失去，无法找回。

19
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:19,560
However, in their article 'Forgetfulness: Why your mind going blank can be a benefit', Sanjay Sarma and Luke Yoquinto write that forgetting, it seems, is "less like a cliff slowly collapsing into the sea, and more like a house deep in the woods that becomes harder and harder to find".
然而，桑杰·萨尔马和卢克·约昆托在他们的文章《健忘：为什么头脑空白可能是一件好事》中写道，遗忘似乎"不太像慢慢坍塌入海的悬崖，而更像是森林深处一座越来越难找到的房子"。
